    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d675">

                <pb facs="0931" n="817" sameAs="frus:frus1931v01/pg817"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">150.071 Control/38</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Assistant Secretary of State</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Castle</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">of a
                        Conversation With the German Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Von
                        Prittwitz</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-03-03">March 3, 1931</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>The German Ambassador called this morning to leave the attached memorandum<anchor
                        n="29" xml:id="d675-f1"/> concerning Senate Bills with regard to shipping. I
                    told the Ambassador that I thought there was comparatively little chance that
                    either of these bills would go through at the present session of Congress, but,
                    of course, there was always a danger. I told him that this Department was
                    opposed to the bills in their present form and that we had already signified to
                    the appropriate people in Congress our disapprobation. He said he realized we
                    had done this, but that he felt he would be derelict, the situation being as it
                    is, not to bring the matter once more to our attention.</p>
                <closer><signed>W[<hi rendition="#small-caps">illiam</hi>] R. C[<hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">astle</hi>], <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Jr.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
